WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.559 --> 00:00:02.392
- [Mark] Okay, so Frank Cullotta.

00:00:02.392 --> 00:00:05.100
Frank, you grew up in Chicago.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.671
- [Frank] That's correct.

00:00:06.671 --> 00:00:11.040
- [Mark] And you had involvement
with the Chicago mob,

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.530
which was called the
Outfit, the Chicago Outfit?

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:15.720
- Yeah, we referred to it as the Outfit.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.170
- [Mark] Yeah, and your father

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:22.060
had a similar kind of path as you, right?

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:26.610
- My father, you know, he
never wanted to be a gangster.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:27.920
He was a thief.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:29.955
He was a killer, you know.

00:00:29.955 --> 00:00:30.788
He was all of them things,

00:00:30.788 --> 00:00:35.116
but he never went in to be
controlled by any organization,

00:00:35.116 --> 00:00:37.715
like the, back then it would
have been the syndicate

00:00:37.715 --> 00:00:38.735
or the outfit.

00:00:38.735 --> 00:00:39.568
- [Mark] Right.

00:00:39.568 --> 00:00:41.276
And tell me about your childhood.

00:00:41.276 --> 00:00:43.260
You grew up with your mom and dad.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.250
- Yeah, I grew up I had a sister.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.980
She was five years older than me.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:51.920
My mother was nine months
pregnant with my brother

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.610
when my father died.

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:57.093
So my brother never
got to meet his father.

00:00:57.093 --> 00:00:57.926
- [Mark] Oh, wow.

00:00:57.926 --> 00:01:00.310
- And she was a very
good mother, at least,

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.660
I know everybody thinks
their mother is the best.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.520
Well, I feel as though
my mother was the best.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.672
Woman visited me every
day, every Sunday in jail

00:01:08.672 --> 00:01:11.953
for as many years as I
did, since I was a kid.

00:01:12.977 --> 00:01:14.052
- [Mark] That's great.

00:01:14.052 --> 00:01:16.437
What, tell me about your father.

00:01:16.437 --> 00:01:19.320
- My father was a, he
was a, not a bank robber,

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.220
but he would do it I guess.

00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.910
He did a lot of robberies and burglaries.

00:01:23.910 --> 00:01:27.937
He was more or less a good
wheel man, getaway driver.

00:01:27.937 --> 00:01:29.490
That's what his reputation was.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:33.080
How I knew all of this stuff
was because of relatives

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.560
used to telling me as I was
growing up, and his friends,

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:36.910
my father's friends.

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:39.100
Otherwise I wouldn't
have known that stuff.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:43.790
I just knew my father when I
was a kid had a violent temper,

00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:47.490
and he was very providing for the house.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:48.610
Very good.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.240
He hollered at my mother a lot.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.363
He was very jealous,
but very much in love.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.554
And I love my father.

00:01:56.554 --> 00:01:58.517
To this day, I still love him.

00:01:58.517 --> 00:01:59.516
- [Mark] He's Italian.

00:01:59.516 --> 00:02:00.811
That explains a lot of it, right?

00:02:00.811 --> 00:02:02.160
- He died in an automobile accident.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:02.993
He was killed.

00:02:02.993 --> 00:02:07.960
He was being pursued by
the police 'cause he had,

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.140
back then they used to
call 'em gas stamps,

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:11.940
rationing stamps.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.918
And he had a carload of 'em.

00:02:13.918 --> 00:02:16.460
He was on, he just had been
sentenced to eight years

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:20.657
in Des Moines, Iowa because
he got caught out there.

00:02:20.657 --> 00:02:21.570
I think he was set up.

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:23.900
So he was out on some
type of an appeal bond,

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.190
and he had more of
these stamps in his car,

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:31.315
and he took off, and he was,
he got killed in the car,

00:02:31.315 --> 00:02:32.315
car accident.

00:02:32.315 --> 00:02:36.310
- [Mark] Hmm, and there's a
connection between your father

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:39.808
and, so you met Tony Spilotro,

00:02:39.808 --> 00:02:42.035
who is a infamous Chicago mobster,

00:02:42.035 --> 00:02:44.183
when you guys were kids, right?

00:02:44.183 --> 00:02:48.790
- I guess Tony Spilotro and
myself met at a very young age.

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:52.240
I am assuming it was about
12 years old or maybe 11,

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:53.872
somewhere around there.

00:02:53.872 --> 00:02:55.620
It's been a long time,
but it was around there.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.580
And my mother finally sent me on.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:00.850
She says, you've gotta start,
you know, go make money.

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:03.300
Here's a shoe box, make a shoe box.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.300
So I made a shoe box, shoe shine box.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.760
Cause I was stealing out
of paper bags before then,

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.200
I was climbing up the pole
and reaching into the bag,

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.487
grabbing nickels out and running.

00:03:14.487 --> 00:03:16.313
Finally, the guy started chasing me.

00:03:16.313 --> 00:03:17.957
- [Mark] That's how they
sold newspapers back then.

00:03:17.957 --> 00:03:20.370
- Newspapers, yeah, there were newspapers.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:21.376
It was on your honor.

00:03:21.376 --> 00:03:22.730
You drop a nickel in and take a paper out.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:26.090
Well, I dropped my hand in
and took all the money out.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:29.030
So I'd, you know, finally
he'd start chasing me.

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.540
So that's when I start shining shoes,

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:34.770
and the main street in
Chicago that I hung on

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.690
and been up and down all my life

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:38.663
is a street called Grand Avenue.

00:03:40.033 --> 00:03:43.048
So I'm on one side of the street,

00:03:43.048 --> 00:03:44.747
and I hear a voice yelling at me.

00:03:44.747 --> 00:03:47.707
"You're in my territory, get out of here,"

00:03:47.707 --> 00:03:49.130
and I look across the street,

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:52.180
and I see a guy that was
a little shorter than me.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:53.710
So he starts walking towards me.

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:55.540
I walk towards him.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.557
He says to me, "This is my territory.

00:03:58.557 --> 00:04:01.450
"You need to get out of here
or I'll bust your head."

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:03.367
And I look up at the
street sign, and I said,

00:04:03.367 --> 00:04:04.200
"I don't see your name.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.430
"What's your name?"

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.160
And he says, "Spilotro."

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:09.770
I said, I don't see your
name on that street sign.

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:11.323
And I'll be back here tomorrow.

00:04:12.857 --> 00:04:14.311
And he said, "Well, when
I come back tomorrow,

00:04:14.311 --> 00:04:15.312
"I'm busting your head."

00:04:15.312 --> 00:04:16.217
I said, "I'll be back."

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:18.023
So the next day I come back,

00:04:18.023 --> 00:04:19.230
the next day I come back for,

00:04:20.145 --> 00:04:21.800
I was there every day for a week or two.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:25.260
And I didn't see this little
guy with this bad attitude.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:30.260
Then one day I heard, hey,
cause he had asked me my name

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.130
when we were arguing on the street.

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:33.732
He said, "What's your name?"

00:04:33.732 --> 00:04:36.120
And I tell him Cullotta, Frank Cullotta.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:38.533
So when I seen him after a week or two,

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:43.067
he yelled to me again,
and we met in the street

00:04:43.067 --> 00:04:44.190
in the middle, it was street cars then.

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:47.423
So we stood by the tracks, and he said,

00:04:48.277 --> 00:04:50.326
"I gotta ask you a question.

00:04:50.326 --> 00:04:52.527
"What was your father's first name?"

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.263
And I said, 'Why?"

00:04:55.263 --> 00:04:58.382
He says, "I just need to ask
you, and I'll tell you why."

00:04:58.382 --> 00:05:00.810
I said, "It was Joe or Joseph."

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.381
He said, "Well, you and
I are gonna be friends

00:05:03.381 --> 00:05:04.450
"for the rest of our lives."

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:06.164
And I said, "Why?

00:05:06.164 --> 00:05:07.250
"What's the reason for that?"

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:10.017
And he said, "My father overheard me

00:05:10.017 --> 00:05:12.119
"talking with my brothers

00:05:12.119 --> 00:05:15.877
"about a guy I was having a
problem with shining shoes

00:05:15.877 --> 00:05:17.497
"in my territory.

00:05:17.497 --> 00:05:20.387
"When he heard the name Cullotta, he says,

00:05:20.387 --> 00:05:22.561
"he called me into the room and he said,

00:05:22.561 --> 00:05:25.167
"find out if that boy's name is,

00:05:25.167 --> 00:05:28.230
"if that boy's father's name is Joe."

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:30.077
He says, "'cause if it is,

00:05:30.077 --> 00:05:33.020
"you and him gotta be the
best friends from now on

00:05:33.917 --> 00:05:36.247
"because his father saved my life."

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:40.420
And that's what Tony told
me in the conversation.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:41.980
And I said,

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.240
then I come to find out what took place

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.990
and how my father saved
Tony's father's life.

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.970
Tony's father's name was Patsy.

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:56.970
Tony's father had a
restaurant on Grand and Ogden.

00:05:57.806 --> 00:05:59.573
We referred to that as
the old neighborhood.

00:06:00.668 --> 00:06:02.263
It's up northwest.

00:06:05.576 --> 00:06:08.900
It was called Patsy's Restaurant,

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:10.730
and he was famous for his sandwiches,

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:13.010
his meatball sandwiches.

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:16.300
What I come to find out
is my father went in there

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:19.530
like everybody else around
that area to get food

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:21.033
and spend money.

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:24.417
Patsy told him one day,
he said, "Hey, Joe,

00:06:24.417 --> 00:06:27.167
"I'm having a lot of problems
with these grease balls."

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:31.226
Grease balls back then
meant the Black Hand.

00:06:31.226 --> 00:06:32.750
That was the only thing around.

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:37.210
There was no Al Capone, no Tony Accardo.

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:40.900
So my father said, "Well, what
are they doing to ya, Patsy?"

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:44.037
And he said, "Well, they
come in, they collect.

00:06:44.037 --> 00:06:46.115
"They wanna collect from me every week.

00:06:46.115 --> 00:06:47.940
"Taxes to stay in business."

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.894
He said, "I can't afford it."

00:06:49.894 --> 00:06:52.200
He said, "I'm just a
little business man here."

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:54.247
My father said, "What day they come in?"

00:06:55.772 --> 00:06:57.234
And I guess he told him the day.

00:06:57.234 --> 00:06:59.940
My father says, "I'll be here
that day before they get here,

00:07:00.787 --> 00:07:01.967
"in your back, the kitchen.

00:07:01.967 --> 00:07:03.457
"Do you usually bring him in the kitchen

00:07:03.457 --> 00:07:04.770
"when you give him money?"

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:06.560
He said, "Yeah."

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:07.960
So my father says, all right.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.283
So the following, whatever the day was,

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.143
he came in with two of his guys,

00:07:14.486 --> 00:07:16.000
and they waited in the kitchen.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.190
So when this guy come to
collect money from Patsy,

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.953
Patsy brought him into the kitchen.

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:25.250
Well, they got him in the kitchen,

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:26.710
my father and his other two friends,

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:27.710
and they killed him.

00:07:28.929 --> 00:07:31.750
Patsy wasn't in the kitchen
when they killed him.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:33.633
Patsy walked out of the kitchen,

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:36.840
and then I don't know how
they disposed of his body.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:38.943
But that's what happened to this guy.

00:07:39.825 --> 00:07:41.725
Well, the word got back to their boss.

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:44.750
No grease balls used to
walk up and down the streets

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:46.523
putting black hands on doors.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:51.045
And he wanted to know who these guys were.

00:07:51.045 --> 00:07:52.840
And they was afraid to go by Patsy's

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:56.180
because he didn't know if
Patsy had any more guys

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:57.720
waiting for him.

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:02.720
So he start continuing his
operation around Grand and Ogden

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:06.700
by putting black hand,
they'd have a black ink hand

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:10.201
put on people's doors, paper,

00:08:10.201 --> 00:08:12.120
and people would know what that meant.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:15.480
So happened to be one
day, my mother come home,

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:19.320
and there was one on our
door, and she looked at it.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:21.010
She didn't know what the hell it meant.

00:08:21.010 --> 00:08:22.377
So when my father comes home, she says,

00:08:22.377 --> 00:08:24.617
"Joe, somebody left
this thing on the door."

00:08:25.924 --> 00:08:29.245
Well, he went crazy, and he, you know,

00:08:29.245 --> 00:08:34.245
and then he found out where
this guy went for, the boss,

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:38.460
but it happened to be his under boss,

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:42.660
the boss's underboss, goes for a haircut.

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:44.060
And he caught him in the barbershop,

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:46.540
and he killed him in the barbershop.

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:49.683
Then he thought to himself,
or this is what I was told,

00:08:51.112 --> 00:08:52.635
he's got to get the head of this guy,

00:08:52.635 --> 00:08:53.900
the head of the organization.

00:08:53.900 --> 00:08:57.699
So by this time, the head of
that organization of black hand

00:08:57.699 --> 00:09:00.220
went in hiding, and he
took his wife with him,

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:03.740
and they found out that he
was in a motel with his wife.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:06.794
They kicked down a door,
and they killed him in bed

00:09:06.794 --> 00:09:08.300
in front of his wife,
didn't hurt his wife.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.730
That was the end of the black hand.

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:15.730
So then soon after that,
the outfit became, you know,

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:19.717
Al Capone and these guys.

00:09:19.717 --> 00:09:21.740
And they wanted my father to be involved,

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:23.260
and he didn't wanna be involved.

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:26.580
He didn't want nothing to do
with any type of organization.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.830
So that was basically
the story of my father.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:30.593
- [Mark] That's a great story.

00:09:30.593 --> 00:09:35.260
So that paved the way for
the mob in Chicago really.

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:38.430
- Oh, yeah, I guess,
yeah, through all Chicago.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.700
See, there's a street in Chicago,

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:43.450
and most of these guys that
were connected to Al Capone

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:46.740
lived on that street in Chicago.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:48.060
It was called Taylor Street.

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:52.043
And that's where, it's called
the 42 Gang come out of.

00:09:52.997 --> 00:09:56.573
That means there was 42 bosses,
42 guys that were made men.

00:09:57.856 --> 00:09:59.058
They'd come out of that area.

00:09:59.058 --> 00:10:01.856
Sam Giancana, Teets Battaglia, every,

00:10:01.856 --> 00:10:03.319
I could go on and on, but I'm not.

00:10:03.319 --> 00:10:04.152
It's too long.

00:10:04.152 --> 00:10:05.520
And they all come out
of that neighborhood.

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:07.398
All old guys, right?

00:10:07.398 --> 00:10:09.690
Now they'd have been over
a hundred some years old.

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:13.536
And Tony Accardo, Tony
Accardo as a matter of fact,

00:10:13.536 --> 00:10:14.910
lived in the house in front of our house

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:17.910
when I was a little child,
maybe four or five years old.

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:21.963
I didn't know that, he was
probably nobody back then.

00:10:23.259 --> 00:10:24.092
I liked Sam Giancana

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:28.180
I would always refer
to him as a man's man.

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:29.950
- [Mark] So you've had interactions

00:10:29.950 --> 00:10:33.900
with so many big and
powerful mobsters from--

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:35.100
- [Frank] I could go on and on

00:10:35.937 --> 00:10:39.300
to the interaction I've had with mobsters.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:41.890
I could be here a week, and
I'm not gonna be here a week.

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:43.779
- [Mark] Just pick two or three

00:10:43.779 --> 00:10:45.016
that you think are
particularly interesting.

00:10:45.016 --> 00:10:46.180
- [Frank] Milwaukee Phil.

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:50.043
I've, Jack Cerone, William Assina

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:53.456
- [Mark] But go, but tell
me about some of the,

00:10:53.456 --> 00:10:56.237
like one or two of these
individuals' personalities.

00:10:56.237 --> 00:10:57.253
- [Frank] Personalities?

00:10:57.253 --> 00:10:58.253
- [Mark] What made them,

00:10:58.253 --> 00:10:59.540
what made them unique and interesting?

00:10:59.540 --> 00:11:01.830
- [Frank] Well, they were all,

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:04.775
each one of them were
unique in their own way.

00:11:04.775 --> 00:11:06.183
You know, they all had tempers.

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:09.573
Some of 'em were narcissistic?

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:12.700
- [Mark] Narcissistic

00:11:12.700 --> 00:11:15.840
- [Frank] And some were
more just the nicest guys

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.140
in the world.

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:20.363
So you had to be careful how
you treated each individual.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:24.960
You'd know what they were
before you even got to meet 'em.

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:28.640
So if you were half smart,
you'd know how to handle 'em,

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:31.578
you know, on a speaking arrangement.

00:11:31.578 --> 00:11:32.595
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:11:32.595 --> 00:11:33.860
- [Frank] Now Milwaukee
Phil, I always felt as though

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:35.573
he growled when he talked.

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.120
Sam DeStefano was a psychopath.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:40.170
I worked for him too.

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:43.690
I've seen him in action.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:46.120
Definitely out of his mind.

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:47.896
Teet's was all right.

00:11:47.896 --> 00:11:50.953
Teet's was wild too, Battaglia.

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:56.343
James Torello, Turk.

00:11:57.472 --> 00:11:58.473
I liked Turk.

00:11:58.473 --> 00:11:59.595
I used to steal with Turk.

00:11:59.595 --> 00:12:00.960
Turk became a boss too.

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:01.793
Joe Ferriola.

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:06.833
We call him Joe Nagall, great guy.

00:12:07.978 --> 00:12:08.811
But you cross these guys,

00:12:08.811 --> 00:12:10.416
you were gonna get fucking whacked.

00:12:10.416 --> 00:12:11.414
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:12:11.414 --> 00:12:12.419
- [Frank] There was no doubt about it.

00:12:12.419 --> 00:12:13.516
You were gonna get whacked

00:12:13.516 --> 00:12:14.410
Like I said, I could go on all day.

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:16.543
I met Tony Accardo two times.

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:19.070
I didn't talk to him on a level

00:12:19.070 --> 00:12:21.650
where he would be like my
going by, "Hey, buddy."

00:12:21.650 --> 00:12:22.483
None of that.

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:27.210
I respected this guy so
much, anything he'd done

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:29.373
because he was a gentleman.

00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:32.810
He never talked down to people.

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:35.710
He knew who I was when I was 18 years old.

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:38.720
When I was 18 years old,
this guy knew who I was.

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:41.190
Shocked me, but that's
how the word travels

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:43.660
in that circle.

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:48.151
When they know a guy's a
tough guy or he's capable,

00:12:48.151 --> 00:12:49.323
they watch you.

00:12:51.277 --> 00:12:52.797
And one time Tony Accardo told me,

00:12:52.797 --> 00:12:55.050
"Hey kid, I know you are."

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:56.143
And I looked at him.

00:12:58.444 --> 00:13:00.344
It was in a pancake house.

00:13:00.344 --> 00:13:01.660
And I said, "Who are you, sir?"

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:04.469
He said, "My name is Anthony Accardo,"

00:13:04.469 --> 00:13:07.091
and I said, "Oh, my God,
it's a pleasure to meet you."

00:13:07.091 --> 00:13:08.172
I was like shocked.

00:13:08.172 --> 00:13:09.440
You know, 'cause I heard about him.

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:11.403
And at that particular time,

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:15.447
Paul Ricca was the boss of the Outfit.

00:13:15.447 --> 00:13:18.783
All right, Paul Ricca was
the boss for about 10 years.

00:13:19.670 --> 00:13:22.706
So he says to me, "I know
all about you, Frankie."

00:13:22.706 --> 00:13:24.306
He says, "You got a bad temper."

00:13:25.461 --> 00:13:27.187
He says, "You could go
a long way," he says,

00:13:27.187 --> 00:13:28.857
"if you control your temper.

00:13:28.857 --> 00:13:30.277
"Just some friendly advice.

00:13:30.277 --> 00:13:32.720
"And by the way, who are your friends?"

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:35.985
He wanted to know who my
friends were next to me.

00:13:35.985 --> 00:13:38.553
So I told him, and the
guy's like, Oh, Mr. Accardo.

00:13:39.778 --> 00:13:41.300
And then the next time I had a stop,

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:46.300
Willie Messino used to work
for, I mean, I'm sorry,

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:50.060
with Accardo by, he used to
do things for Accardo's house.

00:13:50.060 --> 00:13:52.060
Accardo had a monkey.

00:13:52.060 --> 00:13:53.770
Tony Accardo had a monkey.

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:56.500
So poor Willie Messino was
put in charge to the monkey

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:00.600
'cause the monkey would
tear up the basement.

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:02.605
So one time I had a stop there

00:14:02.605 --> 00:14:03.933
and pick up something from Willie,

00:14:05.224 --> 00:14:07.163
and Willie was saying,
"This fucking monkey."

00:14:07.163 --> 00:14:08.724
He says, "I'd kill him," he says,

00:14:08.724 --> 00:14:10.147
"if I could get away with it."

00:14:10.147 --> 00:14:12.980
And then Tony come home,
Tony Accardo come home,

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:15.203
and he looked at me, and he waved,

00:14:15.203 --> 00:14:16.282
and I said, "Hello, Mr. Accardo."

00:14:16.282 --> 00:14:17.290
Always Mr. Accardo.

00:14:17.290 --> 00:14:19.587
He says, "How's my monkey doing, Willie?"

00:14:20.584 --> 00:14:21.920
Willie, looked, shakes his head like that.

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:23.857
I left, I said, "I gotta get out of here."

00:14:23.857 --> 00:14:26.906
But yeah, I know, that's
the only two times

00:14:26.906 --> 00:14:28.170
I talked to the man.

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:30.104
- [Mark] Tell me about Larry Neumann,

00:14:30.104 --> 00:14:32.105
who was somebody you also worked with.

00:14:32.105 --> 00:14:34.443
- Larry Neumann, strange
that you say that.

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:37.617
They talked about a lot of
vicious guys in the Outfit.

00:14:37.617 --> 00:14:42.617
And there were like LaPietra,
LaPietra, his brother, Jimmy.

00:14:43.902 --> 00:14:47.560
Vicious, crazy bastard, scary guy.

00:14:47.560 --> 00:14:50.400
I think Larry Neumann was worse

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:53.380
than anyone of the worst
they had in the Outfit.

00:14:53.380 --> 00:14:55.013
He was a Jewish guy.

00:14:55.900 --> 00:14:59.483
I met him in a penitentiary,
Stateville penitentiary.

00:14:59.483 --> 00:15:03.010
He was doing a buck and
a quarter, it's 125 years

00:15:03.010 --> 00:15:05.190
for a triple murder.

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:07.323
He did 11 and a half years on it.

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:11.160
His father paid off the parole board.

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:13.670
His father was very wealthy.

00:15:13.670 --> 00:15:15.020
So that's where I met Lefty.

00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:17.530
We worked in an assignment together.

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:20.293
They called it the detention hospital.

00:15:21.940 --> 00:15:24.230
They refer to those guys as the goon squad

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:26.743
because we would do

00:15:26.743 --> 00:15:28.320
what the guards didn't
have the balls to do,

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:30.580
get crazy people out of the cells

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:33.500
and bring 'em to this detention hospital

00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:35.630
where all the psychos were.

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:36.750
And that's who we would watch.

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:38.040
There was eight of us.

00:15:38.040 --> 00:15:41.020
So we control that
floor, that hospital unit

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:42.750
with the psychos.

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:43.730
And that's where I met him.

00:15:43.730 --> 00:15:48.303
And then Larry joined me
in later years in Vegas.

00:15:49.824 --> 00:15:52.168
- [Mark] But he was, he was
a killer that took it to--

00:15:52.168 --> 00:15:53.217
- He was what?

00:15:53.217 --> 00:15:54.050
- [Mark] He was a killer,

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:54.883
but he took it to another level, right?

00:15:54.883 --> 00:15:59.360
- I referred to Larry
Neumann as a serial killer.

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:01.863
He enjoyed killing, and it
didn't make any difference

00:16:01.863 --> 00:16:02.696
how he killed you.

00:16:02.696 --> 00:16:04.780
If he had to cut you up in
pieces to get rid of ya,

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:06.270
he could do that.

00:16:06.270 --> 00:16:08.910
You know, I mean, the guy
had, he was just an evil man.

00:16:08.910 --> 00:16:10.013
He was an evil man.

00:16:10.964 --> 00:16:12.273
He died in the penitentiary.

00:16:13.670 --> 00:16:16.420
He went back to the, I put
him back in the penitentiary

00:16:17.277 --> 00:16:20.365
because he, I testified him
on a murder he committed,

00:16:20.365 --> 00:16:21.453
which he shouldn't have committed.

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:26.260
- [Mark] So, so eventually
you ended up doing some,

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:30.744
like you started off with some robberies,

00:16:30.744 --> 00:16:33.344
like a bank and Brinks
trucks and stuff like that?

00:16:33.344 --> 00:16:34.366
- Who was that?

00:16:34.366 --> 00:16:35.365
- [Mark] You did, you.

00:16:35.365 --> 00:16:36.363
- Me?

00:16:36.363 --> 00:16:37.362
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:16:37.362 --> 00:16:38.383
- Yeah, you gotta start somewhere,

00:16:38.383 --> 00:16:39.382
and I started out.

00:16:39.382 --> 00:16:41.442
- [Mark] But you started
out big. (Mark laughing)

00:16:41.442 --> 00:16:42.603
These were big.

00:16:42.603 --> 00:16:44.705
- Well, no, well, I got big quick.

00:16:44.705 --> 00:16:49.705
I was, I had a car I bought
with stocks and bonds

00:16:50.005 --> 00:16:51.010
that my father left.

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:53.943
So it was an Oldsmobile, nice.

00:16:53.943 --> 00:16:57.030
98 it was 55 or so, I
don't know what it was,

00:16:57.030 --> 00:16:59.730
but it was an older car,
it wasn't brand new.

00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:01.301
Real old.

00:17:01.301 --> 00:17:03.780
So I was in the alley doing
something by my house,

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:05.157
in Chicago doing something,

00:17:05.157 --> 00:17:07.520
and this guy come walking down the alley.

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:09.140
He had a fedora on like this.

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:10.483
He was tall and skinny.

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:14.860
We refer to him in later
years as Mopey Jesus.

00:17:14.860 --> 00:17:16.760
That's what we named him, Mopey Jesus.

00:17:17.846 --> 00:17:19.833
His name was Bob Spirota.

00:17:21.625 --> 00:17:23.800
And he stopped, and he's talking to me.

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:26.785
And he's got a wad of
money, showing me his money.

00:17:26.785 --> 00:17:28.220
I said, "Where did you
get all that money?"

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:31.457
And he said, "You wanna
make money like me?

00:17:31.457 --> 00:17:32.290
"I'll show ya."

00:17:33.583 --> 00:17:35.750
He says, "I need a guy
with a car, " he says.

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:38.002
I says, "What do you do?"

00:17:38.002 --> 00:17:39.550
He says, "I stick up taverns."

00:17:39.550 --> 00:17:41.024
Stick up taverns.

00:17:41.024 --> 00:17:42.145
What did I know?

00:17:42.145 --> 00:17:42.978
Stick up taverns, yeah.

00:17:42.978 --> 00:17:45.630
He says, "I'm gonna start doing banks."

00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:48.598
I says, "I don't wanna do banks."

00:17:48.598 --> 00:17:49.431
He says, "You wanna come with me?"

00:17:49.431 --> 00:17:51.230
I say, "Yeah, I'll give it a try."

00:17:51.230 --> 00:17:53.302
But I wear these glasses.

00:17:53.302 --> 00:17:54.135
I had thick glasses then.

00:17:55.290 --> 00:17:56.547
He says, "Take 'em off."

00:17:57.796 --> 00:17:59.477
I said, "I don't know, them
places are dark at night."

00:17:59.477 --> 00:18:00.950
I give it a shot.

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:05.950
So I met him, and we went
on our first tavern heist.

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:10.370
It was a fucking nightmare.

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:12.500
It was so dark in there I couldn't see.

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:15.716
He went in there, and he
shot, he blew off the bottles

00:18:15.716 --> 00:18:18.810
off the black bar, you know,
off the back end of the bar.

00:18:18.810 --> 00:18:21.920
Everybody hit the ground
like they were supposed to.

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:23.518
I'm yelling.

00:18:23.518 --> 00:18:24.517
It's dark.

00:18:24.517 --> 00:18:25.533
I can't see in there.

00:18:25.533 --> 00:18:26.920
I'm yelling to this image.

00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:29.230
It was a top coat and a hat,

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:31.290
get down, and I'm yelling, get down.

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:33.556
And Bob said, "Who you yelling at?"

00:18:33.556 --> 00:18:35.370
"At that jag off that don't
wanna lay down over there,"

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:36.510
and Bob's laughing.

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:37.970
He goes over, and he hits it.

00:18:37.970 --> 00:18:42.970
It was a coat rack, and
the hat was set onto

00:18:43.715 --> 00:18:44.548
where the top coat.

00:18:44.548 --> 00:18:45.381
And it's dark in there.

00:18:45.381 --> 00:18:46.950
I'm telling you, I couldn't see it

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:50.000
'cause I had bad vision.

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.140
So we laughed all the way
to the car, and he said,

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:55.121
I told him, "If you ever
tell anybody about this,

00:18:55.121 --> 00:18:56.161
"I'll kill ya."

00:18:56.161 --> 00:18:57.030
I told him.

00:18:57.030 --> 00:19:00.430
And then from there, I
didn't go with him too often

00:19:00.430 --> 00:19:02.423
'cause he was a little wild.

00:19:03.290 --> 00:19:05.663
And then I hooked up with two other guys,

00:19:06.505 --> 00:19:08.203
Richard Gorman and Paulie Schiro.

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:12.463
And we start the, and Gino,
a guy Gino Chiappetta.

00:19:14.261 --> 00:19:17.070
And we start, we did a couple taverns.

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:19.224
One was in Cicero.

00:19:19.224 --> 00:19:21.520
Should've never went
there, had a shoot out.

00:19:21.520 --> 00:19:23.060
And it was a bad shoot out.

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:24.721
We got away.

00:19:24.721 --> 00:19:26.400
But one of the bullets went
through the windshield,

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:29.440
and parted my partner's
hair Gino Chiappetta.

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:32.433
Left a part down the center
of his hair, legitimate.

00:19:33.490 --> 00:19:35.740
So he says, that's it, but on the way,

00:19:35.740 --> 00:19:38.470
we caught a rumble from the cops,

00:19:38.470 --> 00:19:40.750
somewhere when we got closer to Chicago.

00:19:40.750 --> 00:19:43.500
We had changed the license
plates and everything,

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:45.430
and it became a shoot out in the car,

00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:47.146
shooting back and forth.

00:19:47.146 --> 00:19:48.040
We got away.

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:50.520
But in that shoot up,
we passed Tony Spilotro

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:53.270
was on a street, a side
street where he lived.

00:19:53.270 --> 00:19:56.320
And he was in the car talking
to a couple of his buddies,

00:19:56.320 --> 00:19:59.106
and we got in back of him 'cause
he was blocking the street,

00:19:59.106 --> 00:20:00.950
and we were being chased.

00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:03.207
And, you know, I start blowing the horn.

00:20:03.207 --> 00:20:05.157
"Move out of the fucking
way," we're yelling.

00:20:05.157 --> 00:20:06.370
I didn't know Tony was in the car.

00:20:06.370 --> 00:20:10.374
So Tony knew something was
up, and he told the guy,

00:20:10.374 --> 00:20:11.317
"Move over there, these guys are,

00:20:11.317 --> 00:20:13.710
"whoever they are, they're being chased."

00:20:13.710 --> 00:20:14.543
They pull over.

00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:17.076
We hit the car, and drew
a couple shots, got away.

00:20:17.076 --> 00:20:21.340
Got rid of the guns and
everything, the car,

00:20:21.340 --> 00:20:22.710
see Tony the following day.

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:24.100
Was that you?

00:20:24.100 --> 00:20:25.550
I said, "Yeah."

00:20:25.550 --> 00:20:27.234
He says, "What the?"

00:20:27.234 --> 00:20:29.634
I said, "Ah, we stuck up
some tavern in Cicero."

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:34.370
He says, there's an
easier way to make money.

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:35.994
I says, "How?"

00:20:35.994 --> 00:20:37.132
He says, "Bank routes."

00:20:37.132 --> 00:20:38.315
I say, "I ain't sticking up no banks.

00:20:38.315 --> 00:20:39.490
"I ain't sticking up no banks."

00:20:39.490 --> 00:20:43.110
He says, "You grab the carrier."

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:45.210
He says they go in, and they get money,

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:47.390
and then they bring it
back to the business.

00:20:47.390 --> 00:20:49.540
He says, "I'm doing it
right now with Turk."

00:20:49.540 --> 00:20:51.940
He was with Torello back then.

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:53.920
That's a long time ago.

00:20:53.920 --> 00:20:57.528
So I says, he says that you
could go with us now and then.

00:20:57.528 --> 00:20:58.361
I said, "All right."

00:20:58.361 --> 00:20:59.580
So I got my two partners,

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:01.920
the guys I mentioned previously,

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:06.920
not Gino, Paulie and Richard Gorman.

00:21:07.260 --> 00:21:08.264
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:21:08.264 --> 00:21:10.160
- So we just went on the bank route,

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:12.563
and we grabbed a few tellers.

00:21:12.563 --> 00:21:14.020
People are creatures of habit.

00:21:14.020 --> 00:21:17.470
They do the same damn thing
every day if they're, you know.

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:19.220
They go to the same stores.

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:21.080
They go to the same banks.

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:23.270
They go to the same businesses.

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:24.927
So we followed them.

00:21:24.927 --> 00:21:25.926
They were very easy.

00:21:25.926 --> 00:21:28.620
A couple of cars, two guys,
stick 'em up in the car.

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:30.250
We used to grab 'em right on the street

00:21:30.250 --> 00:21:31.493
walking out of the bank.

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:35.080
You know, $20,000, $15,000.

00:21:35.080 --> 00:21:39.207
That's a lot of money
back then, a lot of money.

00:21:39.207 --> 00:21:40.040
- [Mark] Right.

00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:43.250
- Now it's like $20, $20,000, right?

00:21:43.250 --> 00:21:45.190
So we grabbed these people.

00:21:45.190 --> 00:21:49.420
So then, we were getting
bigger and bigger,

00:21:49.420 --> 00:21:51.143
making bigger and bigger money,

00:21:52.290 --> 00:21:55.160
but we wanted to stop
doing them bank robberies

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:58.103
because they would start
laying traps for ya.

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:01.160
So we did one in Wisconsin.

00:22:01.160 --> 00:22:03.002
That was the last one.

00:22:03.002 --> 00:22:04.366
And then we said, that's it.

00:22:04.366 --> 00:22:06.110
We won't do no more 'cause
the cops are wait for you.

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:08.586
And eventually they're gonna catch you.

00:22:08.586 --> 00:22:11.330
They didn't have all them
cameras like they got now.

00:22:11.330 --> 00:22:14.840
So then we started doing
burglaries, house burglaries,

00:22:14.840 --> 00:22:16.240
but they was all tips.

00:22:16.240 --> 00:22:18.593
Everything we got was inside information.

00:22:19.690 --> 00:22:23.063
And then I went to jail.

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:26.853
Shit thing I went to jail for.

00:22:28.143 --> 00:22:33.120
When I got outta jail, I was 19.

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:35.913
I went in in 1959, I believe.

00:22:35.913 --> 00:22:37.273
I got out in '60.

00:22:38.407 --> 00:22:39.960
It was only a year.

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:42.100
It was in the House of
Corrections, so I had a,

00:22:42.100 --> 00:22:46.370
I had a felony reduced to a misdemeanor.

00:22:46.370 --> 00:22:48.790
That's why I was, two
